    "title": "How to Watch and See the Artemis II Moon Launch",
    "text": "AdvertisementSKIP ADVERTISEMENTThe first crewed mission around the moon in more than 50 years is coming up. Here’s how to see it at sites in and around the Kennedy Space Center in Florida or online.NASA is preparing to send four astronauts on its lunar Artemis II mission. Above, the scene on Jan. 17 during the rollout at the Kennedy Space Center in Florida.Credit...Cassandra Klos for The New York TimesPublished Jan. 28, 2026Updated March 30, 2026March 27: This article was updated to include a launch schedule change announced by NASA.In one of the most anticipated space missions of the decade, NASA is preparing to send four astronauts on a flyby of the moon during its Artemis II mission. If successful, it will be the first crewed flight to venture around the moon since the last Apollo mission more than 50 years ago.Artemis II will take off from Launch Pad 39B on the northern end of the Kennedy Space Centeron Merritt Island, near Titusville,Fla. The 322-foot Space Launch System rocket and Orion capsule in which the astronauts will fly was supposed to launch in February. But in order to address technical problems with the vehicle, NASA pushed back the launch date several times before returning the rocket to the launch site earlier this month.The mission is now expected to begin counting down to launch as soon as April 1, depending on weather conditions, with flight opportunities extending through April 6.Thinking of traveling to the Space Coast for the event? Here’s what you need to know.How do I buy tickets?You may have a hard time.Tickets for viewing packages were released by the Kennedy Space Center and for purchase directly through the center’s website.The “Feel the Heat” package, which offers the closest viewing point at 3.5 miles from the launchpad, sold out within hours. As of Thursday, tickets for viewings at the privately run main visitor complex eight miles away were also sold out.Subscribe to The Times to read as many articles as you like.Ceylan Yeğinsu is a travel reporter for The Times who frequently writes about the cruise industry and Europe, where she is based.Related ContentAdvertisementSKIP ADVERTISEMENT",
